The compiler defines the template directives and their meanings for every
builder. A directive applies to the construction whose head contains it. Use
commas between expressions, directives and a trailing `if` or `loop`. An
operand belongs to the directive immediately before it. Write `$code "moth"`
without parentheses or an equals sign.

Each directive name may appear once per head. A head accepts one formatter.
A slot declaration and a note each need the whole head. A presence guard may
share its head only with one formatter. An insert may share its head with
other compatible items and names exactly one destination.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    card #= [$html:<aside>[$slot summary]</aside><p>[$slot]</p>]
    example = [card:[$in summary, $md:Build **passed**]Ready to ship.]
]

Here `$in summary` chooses the destination and `$md` formats the content sent
there. A second formatter or a second `$in` in the same head would be an
error. To fill another slot, write another insert.

### Comments and source annotations

`$note` takes no operand and requires a balanced body. It can appear only as
a child of a template body. The compiler skips the contents without evaluating
them. A note contributes no output, establishes no receiving type and supplies
no slot. It cannot stand alone as a value or an entry fragment.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    prompt = [:Run [$note: [old_command] used to go here.]tests]
]

The note can contain balanced brackets. This example produces `Run tests`
without looking up `old_command`.

Source code uses `$doc` to document a declaration and `$todo` to annotate
a following declaration or statement. Source files and packages cannot add
template directives. Builders can register destination names for root inserts.
See @../directives (Source directives) for source attachment and diagnostics.
